# Deny by default, then allow what we know.
#
# This repo sits next to cluster directories holding pull secrets,
# kubeconfigs, kubeadmin passwords and ccoctl signing keys. An
# allow-list is the only arrangement where forgetting to add a rule
# fails safe: a new kind of secret is ignored until somebody
# deliberately permits it, rather than committed until somebody
# notices.
#
# Adding a file to the repo now takes a line here. That is the point.

*

# Descend into directories, or the allowances below can never match.
# Files inside still have to be allowed individually.
!*/

# Tooling and docs.
!.envrc
!.gitignore
!Makefile
!README.md
!flake.lock
!flake.nix
!go.mod
!go.sum

# Scripts, named one by one. A glob like !aws-* looks tidier and is
# wrong: it also matches aws-scratch-secret.json, aws-notes.txt and
# whatever else you leave lying around with that prefix, which defeats
# the entire arrangement. Adding a script costs a line here.
!lib.bash
!aws-lib.bash
!gcp-lib.bash
!aws-create-cluster
!aws-create-install-config
!aws-create-route-servers
!aws-delete-route-servers
!aws-destroy-cluster
!aws-install-saml
!aws-list-route-servers
!aws-login
!gcp-create-cluster
!gcp-create-install-config
!gcp-destroy-cluster
!gcp-installer-credentials
!get-openshift-install

# Go sources, wherever they live under cmd/.
!*.go

# Re-denied after the allowances above, because the last matching rule
# wins and some of these would otherwise be caught by !aws-*.
#
#   clusters/  install-config.yaml with the pull secret inlined,
#              auth/kubeconfig, auth/kubeadmin-password, and the
#              ccoctl service account signing key
#   bin/       openshift-install and ccoctl, hundreds of megabytes
#   aws-credential-process
#              the binary go build drops here, named like a script
/clusters/
/bin/
/.direnv/
/aws-credential-process
